  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA shortwave trough and surface cold front will slowly advance\nacross the Upper Ohio Valley region today. The surface front is\nexpected to drift southward through the day, with showers and a\nfew thunderstorms beginning this morning from Pittsburgh and\npoints north. Shower and thunderstorm chances will gradually \nshift southward later in the day with the crossing front. \n\nElevated instability was in place ahead of the front, so\nincreased the chances for thunder this morning. Instability is\nprogged to become surface based this afternoon south of the\nfront, especially near and south of the I-70 corridor. ML CAPE\nis progged to be from 500-750 j/kg this afternoon along this\ncorridor, with 0-6km shear around 35kt. The Storm Prediction\nCenter has continued to highlight the I-70 corridor and points\nsouth in a Marginal Risk, or level 1 out of 5, for severe storms\nwith damaging wind gusts. The main limiting factor would likely\nbe any cloud cover that limits surface heating, along with a\nrelatively brief window for this damaging wind gust potential. \n\nThe front is expected to exit the region to the south tonight,\nwith decreasing shower chances. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA digging shortwave trough and deepening surface low are both\nexpected to advance across the Midwest region Thursday. The low\nis expected to pull the cold front back north as a warm front\nlater in the day, with increasing shower chances late Thursday\nand Thursday night. There could also be a few thunderstorms with\nelevated instability in place. \n\nModels have trended toward a more amplified trough, resulting in  \na slower FROPA on Friday. The surface low is progged to track\nacross the Lower Great lakes region Friday, pulling a strong\ncold front across the region from late morning into the\nafternoon. Showers and possibly a thunderstorm are expected\nalong and ahead of the front. Strong wind flow aloft, mixing, \nand a tight pressure gradient should result in strong wind \ngusts across the region. The latest NBM probabilities for wind \nadvisory criteria gusts (greater than 45 mph) have decreased \nsomewhat across the lower terrain areas, with most areas at 40 \npercent or less. It still appears the Laurel Highlands and \nhigher terrain areas across WV have the highest potential for \nWind Advisory criteria wind, generally at 60-90 percent. \nRegardless, it will be windy across the entire region with \nmixing in cold advection after FROPA. Wind should begin to \ndiminish late Friday night into Saturday as the pressure \ngradient relaxes.\n\nThe crossing upper trough and some lake enhancement should bring\nrain showers into Friday night, but these should taper off\nSaturday morning under building high pressure. Current progs of\nthe 850 mb temperature range from -3 to -6 deg C by Saturday \nmorning, when a few snow flakes could mix with any showers in\nthe ridges and areas north of I-80. \n\nMuch colder temperatures are expected Friday night through\nSaturday night. Wind will likely preclude frost Friday night,\nthough there could be a few outlying areas that are near\nfreezing. A more favorable night for frost and freeze conditions\nis expected to be Saturday night, where diminishing wind and\nclearing skies should result in readings near or below freezing\nacross much of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe trough is expected to exit eastward by Sunday, with a less\namplified flow aloft into next week. Surface high pressure and\nrising 500 mb heights should result in dry and warmer conditions\nfrom Sunday into next week.",
